  "query_preview": "..\n\nPotent shortwave energy rotating around an amplified mid-level wave \ndrives a sneaky setup for pockets of intense snowfall in the Thumb \nthis evening. Lake moisture has worked in behind the passage of a \n700-850mb cold front, marked by increasing clouds and cellular \nrain/snow showers working in from the northwest. The sharp upper \nwave sends a tropopause fold down to ~600mb over the Thumb this \nevening, causing mid-level lapse rates to steepen considerably in \nthis area. A transient moist absolutely unstable layer (MAUL) has \nbeen evident in several cycles of hi-res soundings between 21z and \n02z, suggestive of marginal convective instability capable of \nproducing localized bursts of moderate to heavy snow. This is shown \nwith ample omega within the DGZ and boundary layer saturation. With \nstill relatively warm surface temps, most areas will see up to a \ndusting on grassy/elevated surfaces. However, localized pockets of \nseveral quick inches of snow will be within reach after sunset given \nthe dynamic lift and instability potentially at play. The bulk of \nactivity is expected north of a line from Owosso to Mt. Clemens, \ntied to the upper cold pool passing aloft. Farther south, a well \nmixed boundary layer and lack of lake moisture favors mostly dry \nconditions. The wave quickly exits east tonight with sharp \nsubsidence in its wake causing most clouds to clear out before \nsunrise. Lows tonight fall into the lower to mid 20s.\n\nChilly conditions tomorrow as the 850mb thermal trough near -10C \nslowly departs, keeping highs mainly in the 30s. High pressure \npasses directly overhead as shortwave ridging crests over the area, \nmaintaining dry conditions. Mostly sunny skies early give way to \nincreasing high cloud late as an upper wave break sends a shortwave \ncascading from southern Alberta down across the lower Missouri \nValley. This will begin to phase with a southern stream wave on \nWednesday, amplifying the ridge over the Great Lakes to produce \nanother dry but mostly cloudy day locally. Southeast return flow \nbrings a warming trend with highs back into the 40s.\n\nBy Thursday, the merger of the upper waves deepens significantly \nover the Tennessee Valley and broad surface low pressure forms off \nthe Carolina coast. This tracks up the east coast into Friday with a \ntrough extending westward into the Ohio Valley and Great Lakes. High \nconfidence exists for widespread precipitation to eventually spread \nnorthward into the local area on Thursday as the comma \nhead/deformation region works through with an easterly feed of \nmoisture via the system's warm conveyor. Onset timing on Thursday \ncarries relatively lower certainty at this stage given preceding dry \nair that will take time to saturate, but a widespread soaking rain \nis likely through Thursday night into Friday. NBM advertises storm \ntotal QPF mean of 1+\" for much of the area, but worth noting that \nthe spread of grand ensemble solution space is high, between 0.25\" \n(25th percentile) in the north and 1.75\" (75th percentile) in the \nsouth.\n\n850mb progs maintain very mild conditions aloft locally as the \ncoldest air is directed west and south of the region, and model \nsoundings give high confidence in rain as the primary p-type daytime \nThursday and Friday. Thursday night and Friday night offer the most \nlikely windows for any wet snowflakes to mix in as the nocturnal \nfreezing level descends toward 1 kft AGL or below. The system \ndeparts the east coast late Saturday with drier conditions ensuing \nthis weekend, but the next trough is quick to work in from the west \nSunday/Monday to bring additional shower chances.",
